<header><p class="enc-kicker"><a href="/encyclopedia/conflicts/15th-century">Conflicts &amp; Resolutions</a> · War</p><h1 class="enc-title">Liège Wars</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1465">1465</a>–<a href="/years/1468">1468</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/15th-century">15th century</a> · <a href="/encyclopedia/wars">All wars</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Liège Wars were a series of conflicts between the Prince-Bishopric of Liège and the Burgundian dukes <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/philip-the-good">Philip the Good</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/charles-the-bold">Charles the Bold</a>, whose relative Louis de <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/house-of-bourbon">Bourbon</a> had been imposed as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bishop">bishop</a>. The Liégeois were defeated at Brustem in 1467, and after a further rising Charles the Bold took and sacked Liège in 1468, largely destroying the city.</p></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Background</h2><p>By the middle of the 15th century <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/philip-the-good">Philip the Good</a>, Duke of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/burgundy">Burgundy</a>, ruled so much of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/low-countries">Low Countries</a> that they were called the Burgundian Netherlands. In 1456 he sought to extend his influence to the Prince-Bishopric of Liège. Through his good relations with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pope-callixtus-iii">Pope Callixtus III</a> he had Prince-Bishop John of Heinsberg deposed and replaced by his 18-year-old nephew Louis de <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/house-of-bourbon">Bourbon</a>, who went on studying at the University of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/leuven">Leuven</a> for seven more years while Philip ruled Liège in practice. Resistance grew around Raes van Heers, who sought the support of King <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/louis-xi">Louis XI of France</a>.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Course of events</h2><p>When Louis de <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/house-of-bourbon">Bourbon</a> finally took up his office in 1465, the States of Liège deposed him, and the populace plundered the duke's <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lands-of-overmaas">Lands of Overmaas</a>. Charles the Bold, Philip's son, defeated Raes van Heers's army of about 4,000 men, mostly civilians, at the Battle of Montenaken on 20 October 1465, and the Peace of Sint-Truiden stripped Liège of its rights and restored the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bishop">bishop</a>. In 1466 Charles punished a rising at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dinant">Dinant</a> by throwing 800 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/burghers">burghers</a> into the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/meuse">Meuse</a> and burning the town.</p><p>After Philip's death in 1467 Liège rose again and drove out the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bishop">bishop</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/burgundians">Burgundians</a>. The help that <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/louis-xi">Louis XI</a> had promised did not come, and the troops of Liège were defeated at the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-brustem">Battle of Brustem</a> on 28 October 1467. The city surrendered on 12 November and became a Burgundian protectorate under Guy of Humbercourt.</p><p>In October 1468 rebels under Jean de Wilde, Vincent de Bueren and Gosuin de Streel retook the city. Charles marched on it with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/louis-xi">Louis XI</a> beside him; at Lantin a militia of 500 was driven into the church and burned alive. On the night of 29–30 October six hundred men of Franchimont attacked the Burgundian camp, hoping to kill the duke and the king, and all were killed. Liège surrendered the next day, hundreds of its people were thrown into the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/meuse">Meuse</a>, and the city is said to have burned for seven weeks.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Aftermath and significance</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/charles-the-bold">Charles the Bold</a> was killed at the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-nancy">Battle of Nancy</a> in 1477, and his daughter <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mary-of-burgundy">Mary of Burgundy</a> renounced her rights over the prince-bishopric on 19 March 1477. Louis de <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/house-of-bourbon">Bourbon</a> was murdered on 30 August 1482 by William de La Marck. Later historians recast the wars as a struggle for local freedom, and the attack of the six hundred Franchimontois was later mythologised as an example of Walloon heroism.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Wars of Liège” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
